# ⭐ LeetCode 75 — Day 23: Removing Stars From a String

**📌 Problem:** [2390. Removing Stars From a String](https://leetcode.com/problems/removing-stars-from-a-string/)
**🏷️ Topics:** String, Stack, Simulation
**⚡ Difficulty:** Medium

---

## 🧠 Intuition

Every `*` cancels out the character that came right before it — and that "most recent, not-yet-cancelled" character is exactly what a **stack** is built to track. Push every regular character on; whenever a `*` shows up, pop the top of the stack instead of pushing anything.

---

## 🛠️ Approach

1. Loop through every character of `s`.
2. If the character is `'*'`, pop the top of the stack — this removes the closest character to its left that hasn't been removed yet.
3. Otherwise, push the character onto the stack.
4. Once the whole string has been processed, the stack holds the remaining characters — but in reverse order (since a stack pops from the top). Pop everything into a `result` string, then reverse it to get the correct final order.

---

## ⏱️ Complexity

- **Time complexity:** `O(n)` — each character is pushed and/or popped at most once, and the final reverse is also `O(n)`.
- **Space complexity:** `O(n)` — for the stack and the result string in the worst case (no stars at all).

---

## 💻 Code

**Language/Format:** C++ — solved using LeetCode's OOP submission format (`class Solution` with a public method), using `stack<char>` and `reverse()` from `<algorithm>`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string removeStars(string s) {
        stack<char> st;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s[i] == '*')
                st.pop();
            else
                st.push(s[i]);
        }

        string result = "";
        while (!st.empty()) {
            result.push_back(st.top());
            st.pop();
        }
        reverse(begin(result), end(result));
        return result;
    }
};
```

---

## 🔑 Key Takeaway

Single characters always need single quotes (`'*'`), never double quotes (`"*"`) — double quotes create a string literal (a pointer), which can't be compared directly to a `char`. Also worth remembering: a stack naturally reverses order as you pop it, so if the original order matters, don't forget the final `reverse()` step.

